{"title":"01 CLI entry point","description":"Trace main.py stages, caching, and in-memory DataFrame handoffs.","nextTour":"02 Ingest: transcripts and demographics","steps":[{"file":"main.py","line":56,"title":"Built-in data paths","description":"`DEFAULT_CORPUS_ROOT` / `DEFAULT_DEMO_EXCEL` point at the study's shared-data mount. Every path (corpus, workbook, and the three output dirs) is overridable by CLI flags defined in `main()` at the bottom."},{"file":"main.py","line":63,"title":"One function, five stages","description":"`run_pipeline(corpus_root, demo_excel, data_dir, results_dir, figures_dir, do_process, do_stats, do_cluster, do_importance, do_visualize)`. Each `do_*` boolean toggles one stage; the CLI maps flags onto them."},{"file":"main.py","line":84,"title":"The two cached frames","description":"`processed_features_all.csv` (one row per participant × task) and `processed_features_unique.csv` (one row per participant). Everything downstream reads these two frames — from disk (cache) or from memory (same run)."},{"file":"main.py","line":88,"title":"Processing or cache?","description":"The caching rule: with `--all` (or no stage flags) the corpus is always re-processed. With specific stage flags and no `--process`, the CSVs are loaded from `data/` when they exist (next step) — processing runs only if they don't."},{"file":"main.py","line":106,"title":"Loading the cache","description":"The cache branch: plain `pd.read_csv` of both frames. A stage-only run (`--stats`, `--cluster`, …) lands here when the CSVs exist."},{"file":"main.py","line":114,"title":"Stage 2 — statistics","description":"Group comparisons (ANOVA/Kruskal–Wallis + effect sizes), then ANCOVA adjusted for age and education (line 121), then MoCA correlations (line 128). Each writes one CSV into `results/`."},{"file":"main.py","line":136,"title":"Stage 3 — clustering","description":"Correlation matrix + distance matrix (line 138), K-Means k=4 with domain names (line 141), hierarchical complete-linkage (line 148), and the elbow/silhouette validation for k = 2…6 (line 154). `cluster_df`/`corr_matrix` are kept for the visualization stage."},{"file":"main.py","line":162,"title":"Stage 4 — classification","description":"`prepare_classification_data` (line 164) builds X/y; Random Forest Gini importance (line 167) ranks features; the three-model benchmark RF vs SVM vs Gradient Boosting (line 174) runs 10-fold CV."},{"file":"main.py","line":180,"title":"Stage 5 — figures","description":"Visualization receives the in-memory frames from earlier stages. Line 182: if clustering was skipped, the correlation matrix is recomputed on the fly so Figure 3 still renders."},{"file":"main.py","line":191,"title":"Importance chart is conditional","description":"The importance bar chart renders only if `--importance` ran in the same invocation — `importance_df` is None otherwise. Everything else renders from cached or recomputed frames."},{"file":"main.py","line":218,"title":"Radar uses the all-tasks frame","description":"`plot_task_radar` gets `df_all` (every task row), not the unique-participants frame — task comparison needs all three picture tasks per person. This is the one deliberate consumer of the all-tasks frame downstream."},{"file":"main.py","line":225,"title":"The CLI itself","description":"`main()` defines the argparse surface: `--all --process --stats --cluster --importance --visualize` plus the five path arguments. Line 241: with no stage flag at all, `run_all` is implied."}]}